= Database =

== Overview ==
A '''database''' is an organized collection of persistent data designed for storage, retrieval, update, and management.

A '''database management system''' (DBMS) is software used to define, access, protect, query, and maintain databases.

Therefore:

<pre>
database ≠ DBMS
</pre>

== Data models ==
Databases can use models such as:

* relational tables;
* documents;
* key–value records;
* graphs;
* column families;
* arrays;
* object-oriented structures.

The relational model is highly influential but is not the definition of database.

== Schema ==
A database schema describes structure, constraints, relationships, and types.

Some systems enforce explicit schemas strongly; others allow flexible or evolving structures.

== Queries ==
A query retrieves or transforms data according to a declarative or procedural specification.

SQL is the dominant relational query language, but databases can expose many other languages and APIs.

== Transactions ==
Transactions group operations into units with consistency and failure semantics.

Classical ACID properties describe atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability, but systems implement different isolation and durability guarantees.

== Indexes ==
Indexes trade storage and update cost for faster access paths.

Choosing indexes depends on query patterns and workload.

== Distributed databases ==
Databases can replicate or partition data across machines.

Distribution introduces coordination, consistency, availability, fault-tolerance, and latency tradeoffs.

== Related concepts ==
[[Data structure]], [[Schema]], [[Metadata]], [[Distributed system]], and [[Consistency (logic)]] clarify database organization, representation, distribution, and the special meaning of database consistency.

